ame"}]}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": [], "type": "tips"}, {"type": "post_instructions", "contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_skills", "contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}]}], "id": 11, "type": "DWS"}], "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n3le01ob11re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Nicholas</strong>: Hello, everybody!</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Irene</strong>: Hello! To start with, Nicholas is going to introduce the concept of data compression.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Nicholas</strong>: Yes! Data compression refers to the process of removing redundant information from data to utilize less space where we store this information. Apart from saving disk space, nowadays we&rsquo;re compressing data to make communication faster. We normally compress heavy files to attach them to the e-mails we send, for example.</p>", "id": 0, "image": "enpe7n3le01ob11re2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "34500", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Irene</strong>: Now, I&rsquo;m going to tell you a bit about how the different compression methods normally work. To easily comprehend how these methods work, it is necessary to understand a common aspect: it is not possible to find characters in equal numbers within a text. I&rsquo;m thinking of an example to illustrate this idea. Let&rsquo;s suppose there is a text in English in front of you. If you observe the number letters e and s and the number of letters j and x there are in it, you are going to realize that j and x are less frequent. To minimize the number of bits needed to encode the text, it is necessary to assign the shortest bit codes to the most frequent characters and the longer codes to the least common characters.</p>", "id": 1, "image": "enpe7n3le01ob11re2img02.jpg"}, {"time": "88000", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Nicholas</strong>: We&rsquo;re going to describe some coding methods now. The Huffman coding is an algorithm that consists of a tree in which there are characters paired in ascending order starting from the least to the most common until the tree is complete.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Irene</strong>: Compared to the Huffman coding, the arithmetic coding is more efficient because it matches the probabilities of the characters to bits in a way that one same bit can represent part of more than one encoded character. Unfortunately, this method is bit slower because of the many calculations it needs.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Nicholas</strong>: Right! Another method to compress is to look for character strings that match some terms there are in a dictionary file. In this method, numbers replace matching strings to really reduce the size of most text files simply because a number is shorter than a word or phrase.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Irene</strong>: The next method is the Lempel-Ziv (LZ) compression method. In contrast to the previous one, this one doesn&rsquo;t use an external dictionary file. It scans the text itself to find text strings and replaces not only words but also prefixes and suffixes.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Nicholas</strong>: The last method we&rsquo;re going to talk about is the one that compresses graphic files. In this method, a number indicating a certain count of pixels replaces the corresponding area that represents a defined color. There are formats such as GIF which don&rsquo;t shrink significantly by compressing them because they are already compressed.</p>", "id": 2, "image": "enpe7n3le01ob11re2img03.jpg"}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n3le01ob11re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "title": "", "id": 1, "type": "audio"}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}], "type": "sena"}